1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,902
This is the build tutorial for the bubble clown costume. Make a gun shape with either hand,

2
00:00:04,962 --> 00:00:09,513
squeeze, and a comet of light runs from your elbow down to your fingertips,

3
00:00:09,573 --> 00:00:14,125
arriving exactly as bubbles start firing out of your fingers. We will build it in thirteen steps.

4
00:00:14,185 --> 00:00:18,737
Follow them in order and you will not have to guess at anything.

5
00:00:20,047 --> 00:00:24,859
Two arms, two identical rigs. Each hand has its own trigger and fires its own arm.

6
00:00:24,919 --> 00:00:29,732
The light strips hide under the glove and the sleeve, so the glow reads as coming from inside the

7
00:00:29,792 --> 00:00:34,604
costume. And each arm comes apart into four modules that unplug from each other.

8
00:00:35,914 --> 00:00:40,934
Here is the sequence you are building. You squeeze. The blower gets a brief over drive pulse to break

9
00:00:40,994 --> 00:00:46,014
static friction, then settles to its run speed. A comet of light launches from the elbow.

10
00:00:46,074 --> 00:00:51,093
About two hundred and fifty milliseconds later the comet lands at your fingertips just as the blower

11
00:00:51,153 --> 00:00:56,173
reaches full speed, and bubbles start. The light travel is the spin up delay.

12
00:00:56,233 --> 00:01:01,253
That is the whole trick. It reads as the light causing the bubbles, instead of reading as lag.

13
00:01:02,563 --> 00:01:07,427
Nothing is soldered end to end across a joint. The pod sits on your upper arm and holds the brain

14
00:01:07,487 --> 00:01:12,352
and the battery. The sleeve holds the forearm strip. The glove holds the hand strip and that hand's

15
00:01:12,412 --> 00:01:17,276
trigger. And the bubbler is its own module. Each one unplugs, which is what lets you get out of the

16
00:01:17,336 --> 00:01:22,200
costume alone, with soapy hands, without dislocating a shoulder.

17
00:01:23,510 --> 00:01:28,763
There are five kinds of connector, and this is deliberate. No two plugs on one arm share both family

18
00:01:28,823 --> 00:01:34,076
and pin count. The three two pin plugs are three different pitches, so none of them will mate with

19
00:01:34,136 --> 00:01:39,389
the others. That is what stops you putting three point seven volts onto a logic pin in a dark room.

20
00:01:40,699 --> 00:01:45,914
This is the one rule you will use constantly. Battery out before you mate or unmate anything.

21
00:01:45,974 --> 00:01:51,188
Feeding data into an unpowered strip pushes current through its input protection diodes,

22
00:01:51,248 --> 00:01:56,463
and that is the classic way to kill pixel zero. Unplug the cell first. Every single time.

23
00:01:57,772 --> 00:02:02,630
Before you pick up the iron. Use the protected cells that came with the kits,

24
00:02:02,690 --> 00:02:07,547
and never charge a damaged or puffy one. It is strapped to your arm. Check polarity with a multimeter

25
00:02:07,607 --> 00:02:12,465
before you connect the battery the first time, because reversed power destroys the brain and the strip

26
00:02:12,525 --> 00:02:17,383
instantly and permanently. You must also be able to kill the arm and get the cell out one handed,

27
00:02:17,443 --> 00:02:22,301
in seconds. That is what the disconnect switch and a tool free cradle lid are for.

28
00:02:22,361 --> 00:02:27,218
If getting the battery out needs two hands or a screwdriver, the pod is not finished.

29
00:02:27,278 --> 00:02:32,136
Know this before the night starts. There are no spare cells. Two came with the two kits and none were

30
00:02:32,196 --> 00:02:37,054
bought, so a flat cell ends that arm. Charge both full that morning and kill the disconnect between

31
00:02:37,114 --> 00:02:41,972
sets. If you ever do carry a loose cell, it goes in a plastic case, never a bag with keys.

32
00:02:42,032 --> 00:02:46,889
The whole can of an eighteen six fifty is its negative terminal, and the thin wrap is all that covers

33
00:02:46,949 --> 00:02:51,807
it. And solder somewhere ventilated.

34
00:02:53,117 --> 00:02:57,411
Steps one through eleven build one complete arm, sealed and ready to wear. Step twelve is the second

35
00:02:57,471 --> 00:03:01,765
arm, which takes about half as long because you will already know what you are doing.

36
00:03:01,825 --> 00:03:06,120
Step thirteen is the costume itself. One thing worth hearing now, before you start.

37
00:03:06,180 --> 00:03:10,474
Heat shrink goes onto the wire before you solder the joint, never after. Everybody learns this the

38
00:03:10,534 --> 00:03:14,828
hard way once.

39
00:03:16,138 --> 00:03:18,508
Step one. Measure your arm.

40
00:03:19,818 --> 00:03:24,304
Put on the glove and the sleeve you are actually going to use, and hold your arm out in the finger

41
00:03:24,364 --> 00:03:28,850
gun pose. Measure elbow to wrist crease, which is usually around twenty five centimetres.

42
00:03:28,910 --> 00:03:33,396
Then measure wrist crease to knuckles across the back of your hand, usually around ten.

43
00:03:33,456 --> 00:03:37,942
And mark the spot on your palm where your middle and ring finger pads land when you squeeze.

44
00:03:38,002 --> 00:03:42,488
That is where the trigger goes. Use a fabric pen.

45
00:03:43,799 --> 00:03:48,802
Now turn those into pixel counts. At sixty LEDs per metre each pixel is one point six seven centimetres.

46
00:03:48,862 --> 00:03:53,865
Divide each measurement by that. For typical arms you get about fifteen pixels on the forearm and

47
00:03:53,925 --> 00:03:58,928
six on the hand. Round down. A slightly short strip is fine. One that does not fit is not.

48
00:03:58,988 --> 00:04:03,991
Then subtract about four centimetres from the forearm figure, because the wrist umbilical and its

49
00:04:04,051 --> 00:04:09,054
connector need to live in the last few centimetres of forearm. Write these numbers down.

50
00:04:09,114 --> 00:04:14,117
You will type them into the firmware in step eight.

51
00:04:15,427 --> 00:04:17,553
Step two. Cut the strip.

52
00:04:18,863 --> 00:04:24,179
The strip has marked cut lines between every pixel, with copper pads either side.

53
00:04:24,239 --> 00:04:29,554
Cut through the middle of the pads, not next to them, so you have pad left on both pieces to solder

54
00:04:29,614 --> 00:04:34,930
to. Cut two pieces, one for the forearm and one for the hand. Then check the little printed arrows.

55
00:04:34,990 --> 00:04:40,305
Data flows elbow to fingertips, so both pieces must have their arrows pointing the same way,

56
00:04:40,365 --> 00:04:45,681
toward your fingers. Tape the elbow end of the forearm piece. That end is pixel zero.

57
00:04:46,991 --> 00:04:50,866
It is worth saying plainly. Getting the arrows backwards is the single most common mistake in this

58
00:04:50,926 --> 00:04:54,801
build, and the fix is unsoldering everything. Check both pieces twice before the iron goes anywhere

59
00:04:54,861 --> 00:04:58,736
near them.

60
00:05:00,046 --> 00:05:04,840
Here is the layout you are aiming for. Fifteen pixels on the forearm, a gap of about eight centimetres

61
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:09,695
at the wrist, then six pixels across the back of the hand. The reason it is split is that your wrist

62
00:05:09,755 --> 00:05:14,549
bends constantly. A single continuous strip across that joint will crack its internal copper traces

63
00:05:14,609 --> 00:05:19,404
within a few hours of wear, and everything past the crack goes dark. The flexible umbilical you build

64
00:05:19,464 --> 00:05:24,258
next absorbs that movement instead.

65
00:05:25,568 --> 00:05:28,194
Step three. Build the wrist umbilical.

66
00:05:29,504 --> 00:05:34,805
The umbilical carries five conductors. The strip's five volts, ground and data,

67
00:05:34,865 --> 00:05:40,165
plus this hand's two trigger wires. Use a J S T S M five pin pigtail pair, and fix this pin convention

68
00:05:40,225 --> 00:05:45,526
now. Pin one five volts, pin two ground, pin three data, pins four and five the trigger.

69
00:05:45,586 --> 00:05:50,886
Keep it identical on both arms, or your spares are not spares.

70
00:05:52,196 --> 00:05:57,094
Sleeve side first. Slide the heat shrink onto every wire now. Solder short silicone leads to the output

71
00:05:57,154 --> 00:06:02,052
end of the forearm piece, five volts, ground and data out, and take those three to pins one,

72
00:06:02,112 --> 00:06:07,010
two and three of one half of the pigtail. Pins four and five get two lengths of thin silicone wire,

73
00:06:07,070 --> 00:06:11,968
long enough to run all the way up the forearm to the elbow. That is the trigger's path to the pod.

74
00:06:12,028 --> 00:06:16,926
Shrink everything down, and seal the exposed strip end with a blob of hot glue or clear silicone.

75
00:06:16,986 --> 00:06:21,884
That is your soap proofing.

76
00:06:23,194 --> 00:06:28,125
Glove side. Solder pins one, two and three of the other pigtail half to the input end of the hand

77
00:06:28,185 --> 00:06:33,117
piece. Five volts to five volts, ground to ground, and pin three to data in.

78
00:06:33,177 --> 00:06:38,108
Data in, not data out. Pins four and five get wire long enough to reach your palm,

79
00:06:38,168 --> 00:06:43,100
ending in the female half of a J S T Z H two pin pigtail. That is where the microswitch will plug

80
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:48,091
in at step five. Shrink and seal, same as before.

81
00:06:49,401 --> 00:06:54,490
Mate the pair about three to four centimetres above the wrist crease, on the forearm,

82
00:06:54,550 --> 00:06:59,638
where the skin barely moves. The crease itself is the worst possible place for a rigid twenty millimetre

83
00:06:59,698 --> 00:07:04,787
plastic body. The glove side wire is the flex element, so leave it long enough to cross the wrist

84
00:07:04,847 --> 00:07:09,936
with a service loop. Keep the whole thing short. Every centimetre between the last forearm pixel and

85
00:07:09,996 --> 00:07:15,085
the first hand pixel is dark arm that the comet has to cross. Eight centimetres is fine.

86
00:07:15,145 --> 00:07:20,233
Fifteen will look like a gap. Measure the finished pixel to pixel distance and write it down.

87
00:07:20,293 --> 00:07:25,382
Then gently tug every joint. Find a weak one now, not inside a sewn glove.

88
00:07:26,692 --> 00:07:32,615
Step four. Build the controller pod. This is the longest step, so take it in pieces.

89
00:07:33,925 --> 00:07:39,159
Start with the shape of the power. One protected eighteen six fifty cell feeds two rails.

90
00:07:39,219 --> 00:07:44,454
The blower motor runs straight off the cell at its native three point seven volts,

91
00:07:44,514 --> 00:07:49,748
which is exactly how the kit was designed. Only the strip and the brain get boosted to five volts,

92
00:07:49,808 --> 00:07:55,043
and that is a light load, about zero point three amps, so a small inexpensive boost module is genuinely

93
00:07:55,103 --> 00:08:00,337
adequate. A master disconnect switch goes first, right at the cell, then a two amp polyfuse,

94
00:08:00,397 --> 00:08:05,632
then everything else. One more part you cannot see on this diagram: a schottky diode between the boost

95
00:08:05,692 --> 00:08:10,926
output and the X I A O's five volt pad. We will come back to why.

96
00:08:12,236 --> 00:08:17,415
These are the core connections. The disconnect switch goes first, then the fuse,

97
00:08:17,475 --> 00:08:22,655
right at the battery. Everything downstream of it shares one common ground.

98
00:08:22,715 --> 00:08:27,894
Data leaves G P I O ten, through the level shifter, through a three hundred and thirty to four hundred

99
00:08:27,954 --> 00:08:33,134
and seventy ohm resistor, and out of the elbow connector. The trigger arrives on G P I O three.

100
00:08:33,194 --> 00:08:38,373
Motor P W M leaves on G P I O four. And G P I O two reads the battery. A note on names,

101
00:08:38,433 --> 00:08:43,612
because it will save you a mistake. We always call a X I A O pin by its G P I O number,

102
00:08:43,672 --> 00:08:48,852
and the board's own printed labels, D ten, D one, D two, D zero, appear only in that silkscreen column.

103
00:08:48,912 --> 00:08:54,091
And the two diodes are called C R one and C R two, never D one and D two, because those are pin names

104
00:08:54,151 --> 00:08:59,331
already. And one small capacitor sits directly across the level shifter's own two power pins.

105
00:08:59,391 --> 00:09:04,570
More on that shortly.

106
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:11,299
One detail that is easy to get wrong. The X I A O's pins only swing to three point three volts,

107
00:09:11,359 --> 00:09:16,777
but most so called logic level MOSFETs are specified fully on at five. At three point three they only

108
00:09:16,837 --> 00:09:22,256
partly open, they dissipate the difference as heat, and the blower runs slow and inconsistent.

109
00:09:22,316 --> 00:09:27,734
The level shifter in your pod has four gates and the strip only uses one. So route G P I O four through

110
00:09:27,794 --> 00:09:33,213
a second gate, exactly the way you route the LED data through the first. The MOSFET then sees a clean

111
00:09:33,273 --> 00:09:38,691
five volt signal, and it costs you nothing. Add a hundred ohm resistor in series with the gate,

112
00:09:38,751 --> 00:09:44,170
and a ten k pulldown from gate to source, so the FET is held off while the board boots.

113
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:50,243
And a warning that will save you an evening. Do not use an I R F five twenty module.

114
00:09:50,303 --> 00:09:55,067
It is the top search result for arduino mosfet module and it is not a logic level part.

115
00:09:55,126 --> 00:09:59,890
Its gate threshold runs up to four volts and it wants about ten to open properly.

116
00:09:59,950 --> 00:10:04,713
It half works, which is much harder to diagnose than not working at all.

117
00:10:06,023 --> 00:10:11,347
First new part. A master disconnect switch, in the cell positive, between the battery plug and the

118
00:10:11,407 --> 00:10:16,731
fuse, so it kills everything downstream including the motor and the boost. Buy one rated at three

119
00:10:16,791 --> 00:10:22,115
amps or more at D C. Most small rockers and toggles are specified for mains A C and far less for D

120
00:10:22,175 --> 00:10:27,498
C, so read the D C line, not the picture. Get one with a rubber boot: it keeps its own seal,

121
00:10:27,558 --> 00:10:32,882
and you can find it by feel in the dark. Mount it so you can reach it through the costume,

122
00:10:32,942 --> 00:10:38,266
one handed, without opening the pod, and label which way is off. It does not replace pulling the cell.

123
00:10:38,326 --> 00:10:43,650
It makes the pod safe to open and the arm safe to unplug in one motion.

124
00:10:44,961 --> 00:10:50,093
Second new part, and this one is easy to miss. The X I A O's five volt pad is wired straight to its

125
00:10:50,153 --> 00:10:55,284
U S B C power pin. There is no diode on the board. So with the cell connected and a U S B cable plugged

126
00:10:55,344 --> 00:11:00,476
in, your boost converter is sitting on the host port, feeding a laptop or a phone charger.

127
00:11:00,535 --> 00:11:05,667
Fit a one N five eight one nine schottky between the boost output and the five volt pad,

128
00:11:05,727 --> 00:11:10,859
banded end toward the X I A O. Current flows boost to X I A O, and nothing flows back.

129
00:11:10,918 --> 00:11:16,050
It costs about three tenths of a volt, so the board sees about four point seven,

130
00:11:16,110 --> 00:11:21,242
comfortably inside its regulator. Only the X I A O goes behind the diode. The strip and the level

131
00:11:21,302 --> 00:11:26,433
shifter stay on the boost directly, at a full five volts.

132
00:11:27,742 --> 00:11:32,902
And a warning about the part everyone leaves out. The ten k pulldown from gate to source is not optional.

133
00:11:32,962 --> 00:11:38,122
Between the moment the board resets and the moment the firmware runs, that gate pin is an input,

134
00:11:38,182 --> 00:11:43,342
and it floats. A floating gate holds whatever charge it last had, so the blower twitches,

135
00:11:43,402 --> 00:11:48,563
or briefly runs, every time you power up or reflash, with the code doing nothing at all.

136
00:11:48,623 --> 00:11:53,783
Bare transistors do not include one. Most modules do not either. Meter gate to source with the board

137
00:11:53,843 --> 00:11:59,003
unpowered: a few k is fine, open means fit your own.

138
00:12:00,313 --> 00:12:05,848
One more thing about that level shifter, and it is the kind of detail that bites you months later.

139
00:12:05,908 --> 00:12:11,443
The chip has four gates. You are using two. The other two are not spare parts.

140
00:12:11,503 --> 00:12:17,038
They are inputs, and a C MOS input must never be left floating. A floating input sits wherever stray

141
00:12:17,098 --> 00:12:22,633
charge leaves it, usually somewhere near the switching threshold. The gate then oscillates,

142
00:12:22,693 --> 00:12:28,229
and the chip dissipates real power doing it. Tens of milliamps instead of microamps,

143
00:12:28,288 --> 00:12:33,824
as heat, continuously, out of a battery strapped to your arm. It also couples noise into the two gates

144
00:12:33,884 --> 00:12:39,419
you actually care about, which is the last thing you want on an LED data line.

145
00:12:39,479 --> 00:12:45,014
So: three A and four A go to ground. Three O E and four O E go to V C C, which disables those outputs,

146
00:12:45,074 --> 00:12:50,609
because O E is active low. And three Y and four Y go nowhere at all. Those are outputs.

147
00:12:50,669 --> 00:12:56,204
Leave them open, and never tie an output to a rail. Four short wires, while the chip is going in.

148
00:12:57,514 --> 00:12:59,996
And one more part, the cheapest in the build, that people leave out because it looks redundant.

149
00:13:00,056 --> 00:13:02,539
A nought point one microfarad ceramic capacitor goes across the level shifter's power pins.

150
00:13:02,599 --> 00:13:05,081
Pin fourteen and pin seven on the D I P package. V C C and ground, on the chip itself,

151
00:13:05,141 --> 00:13:07,623
not near it. Here is why it is not the same as the big capacitor you already have.

152
00:13:07,683 --> 00:13:10,166
Every time a gate switches, the chip pulls a burst of current out of its supply pin in a few billionths

153
00:13:10,226 --> 00:13:12,708
of a second. The one thousand microfarad sits by the elbow connector, and between it and the chip

154
00:13:12,768 --> 00:13:15,250
is a run of protoboard wire. Electrically that is a long way. It cannot deliver charge that fast.

155
00:13:15,310 --> 00:13:17,793
So the little ceramic sits right at the pins and holds just enough charge for one edge.

156
00:13:17,853 --> 00:13:20,335
Bulk storage at the strip, decoupling at the chip. Different jobs. Fit both.

157
00:13:20,395 --> 00:13:22,877
It is not polarised, so it goes in either way round. And cut the legs short before you solder it,

158
00:13:22,937 --> 00:13:25,420
because on thirty millimetres of leg it is mostly wire, and it does much less than you think.

159
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:27,962
Skip it and you get a build that works on your bench and misbehaves on your arm.

160
00:13:29,272 --> 00:13:35,903
Everything the pod sends down the arm leaves through exactly two plugs. A six pin at the elbow for

161
00:13:35,963 --> 00:13:42,593
the strip and the trigger, and a two pin for the motor. Pin six is not a spare,

162
00:13:42,653 --> 00:13:49,284
it is a job. The elbow run is the longest in the costume and carries the whole strip's current.

163
00:13:49,344 --> 00:13:55,975
A second ground conductor cuts the voltage drop and gives the data line a better return path.

164
00:13:56,035 --> 00:14:02,666
Tie it to common ground at both ends. And the motor gets its own plug on purpose.

165
00:14:02,726 --> 00:14:09,356
Roughly an amp of switched P W M bundled against a data line is asking for flicker.

166
00:14:09,416 --> 00:14:16,047
Twist the motor pair.

167
00:14:17,357 --> 00:14:20,051
The battery monitor is three connections. Two one hundred k resistors in series across the cell,

168
00:14:20,111 --> 00:14:22,805
and the midpoint goes to D zero. That midpoint sits at exactly half the battery voltage,

169
00:14:22,865 --> 00:14:25,559
which is safely inside what the brain can measure. The firmware does two things with that reading.

170
00:14:25,619 --> 00:14:28,313
Below three point four volts the elbow pixel pulses red, slowly, as a warning to swap soon.

171
00:14:28,373 --> 00:14:31,068
There is a hundred and fifty millivolt band before that clears again, so it does not strobe on and

172
00:14:31,128 --> 00:14:33,822
off every time the motor loads the cell. And below three volts, held for several seconds,

173
00:14:33,882 --> 00:14:36,576
the arm shuts itself down: the motor cuts, the trigger stops working, and the elbow pixel double blinks.

174
00:14:36,636 --> 00:14:39,330
The cell's own protection board is the backstop, not the plan. Repeatedly hauling a lithium cell down

175
00:14:39,390 --> 00:14:42,084
to its protection cutoff is what kills it.

176
00:14:43,394 --> 00:14:49,710
Two parts here are easy to fit backwards. The one thousand microfarad capacitor goes across the strip's

177
00:14:49,770 --> 00:14:56,087
five volts and ground, physically close to the elbow connector. Watch its polarity.

178
00:14:56,147 --> 00:15:02,463
The marked stripe is the negative leg. The one N five eight one nine diode goes directly across the

179
00:15:02,523 --> 00:15:08,840
motor's two terminals at the blower end, and its banded end goes to the positive side.

180
00:15:08,900 --> 00:15:15,216
Backwards, it is a dead short. Check that one twice.

181
00:15:16,526 --> 00:15:16,857
Four rules worth taping inside the pod. Everything shares one common ground.

182
00:15:16,917 --> 00:15:17,249
Skipping this causes bizarre, hard to diagnose behaviour. Keep the cell on its factory P H two plug,

183
00:15:17,309 --> 00:15:17,640
because the kit's charger mates to the same connector, so a flat cell goes pod to charger with no

184
00:15:17,700 --> 00:15:18,031
adapter. Never put anything on D eight or D nine, they are boot strapping pins.

185
00:15:18,091 --> 00:15:18,423
And design the printed pod so you can reach the U S B C port without taking anything apart.

186
00:15:18,483 --> 00:15:18,814
Use a data capable cable. A charge only cable looks identical and will cost you half an hour.

187
00:15:20,124 --> 00:15:52,088
Step five. Wire this hand's trigger.

188
00:15:53,398 --> 00:16:00,584
Solder two lengths of thin silicone wire to the microswitch's common and normally open terminals,

189
00:16:00,644 --> 00:16:07,831
and terminate them in the male half of a Z H two pin pigtail. That is the mate to the one you left

190
00:16:07,891 --> 00:16:15,077
on the glove side in step three. Mount the switch on a small printed plate,

191
00:16:15,137 --> 00:16:22,323
positioned so the lever sits under your middle and ring finger pads, at the spot you marked in step

192
00:16:22,383 --> 00:16:29,569
one. Orient the lever so a natural squeeze presses across its length. You should be able to fire it

193
00:16:29,629 --> 00:16:36,816
with your eyes closed, in one motion, every time. If you find yourself aiming,

194
00:16:36,876 --> 00:16:44,062
move the plate.

195
00:16:45,372 --> 00:16:45,691
Two reasons this tiny plug exists. First, a soldered in switch can only be replaced with a soldering

196
00:16:45,751 --> 00:16:46,070
iron, but one on a pigtail is a ten second swap. Worth knowing: you own exactly two switches,

197
00:16:46,130 --> 00:16:46,448
one per arm, both in use. They came in the bubble kits and no spares were bought,

198
00:16:46,508 --> 00:16:46,827
so right now a dead trigger ends that arm even with the pigtail fitted. The pigtail is what makes

199
00:16:46,887 --> 00:16:47,206
a spare worth carrying the day you buy one. They come in cheap ten packs. Second,

200
00:16:47,266 --> 00:16:47,585
and more important, it must not be P H two point zero. Every cell in this build ships on a P H two

201
00:16:47,645 --> 00:16:47,964
lead. If the trigger used the same connector, one wrong plug in a dark room puts three point seven

202
00:16:48,024 --> 00:16:48,342
volts directly onto a logic pin. Z H is one point five millimetre pitch, P H is two.

203
00:16:48,402 --> 00:16:48,721
They will not mate. That incompatibility is the entire reason for the choice,

204
00:16:48,781 --> 00:16:49,100
so do not simplify it away later.

205
00:16:50,410 --> 00:18:04,972
Step six. Bench test, before anything goes into a glove.

206
00:18:06,282 --> 00:18:08,415
Lay the whole thing out flat on the table, fully wired, every connector mated,

207
00:18:08,475 --> 00:18:10,608
nothing mounted yet. Before any power at all, do two checks. Meter gate to source on the MOSFET:

208
00:18:10,668 --> 00:18:12,802
you want a few k, not open. Open means the pulldown is missing. And check both diodes.

209
00:18:12,862 --> 00:18:14,995
D one's banded leg to motor positive, at the blower end. D two's banded leg to the X I A O.

210
00:18:15,055 --> 00:18:17,188
D one backwards is a dead short across the cell through the MOSFET, so check that one twice.

211
00:18:17,248 --> 00:18:19,381
Then beep through every connector, pin by pin. A crimp that did not seat looks perfect and works intermittently.

212
00:18:19,441 --> 00:18:21,574
Find that now, not at the venue. Now power. Battery plus and minus where you expect them,

213
00:18:21,634 --> 00:18:23,768
and set the boost to five point zero zero volts on its trimpot before the brain is ever connected.

214
00:18:23,828 --> 00:18:25,961
Then confirm the isolation diode: boost output about five volts, X I A O pad about four point seven.

215
00:18:26,021 --> 00:18:28,154
That three tenths of a volt step is the diode. The same reading on both sides means you shorted past

216
00:18:28,214 --> 00:18:30,347
it. Power it up. You should see the dim breathing idle glow, and the blower must not twitch.

217
00:18:30,407 --> 00:18:32,541
If it kicks, stop: floating gate. Press the microswitch by hand. The comet should launch from the

218
00:18:32,601 --> 00:18:34,734
elbow end and travel toward the fingertips, and the motor should spin. Let it run five minutes and

219
00:18:34,794 --> 00:18:36,927
check for heat.

220
00:18:38,237 --> 00:18:44,031
If the comet runs backwards, from the fingertips to the elbow, your strip pieces are reversed.

221
00:18:44,091 --> 00:18:49,884
Fix that in the wiring, not in the code. Both arms run byte identical firmware and keeping that true

222
00:18:49,944 --> 00:18:55,738
is worth the resolder. On heat, warm is fine, too hot to touch is not. Power down and look for a short.

223
00:18:55,798 --> 00:19:01,592
If you can borrow a thermal camera, use it here instead of your fingertip. It shows you a boost module

224
00:19:01,652 --> 00:19:07,445
running hot because something downstream is shorted, or a MOSFET that turned out not to be logic level.

225
00:19:07,505 --> 00:19:13,299
One borrowed session is plenty.

226
00:19:14,610 --> 00:19:14,821
One more bench test, worth doing exactly once per arm so you recognise it in the field and know it

227
00:19:14,881 --> 00:19:15,091
works. Feed the pod two point nine five volts from a bench supply in place of the cell.

228
00:19:15,151 --> 00:19:15,362
Within about six seconds the elbow pixel starts a slow double blink, the motor stops,

229
00:19:15,422 --> 00:19:15,633
and the trigger does nothing. Wind it up to three point seven and it comes back.

230
00:19:15,693 --> 00:19:15,904
A single slow pulse is the warning: swap soon. A double blink is the shutoff:

231
00:19:15,964 --> 00:19:16,174
swap now. They are deliberately different at a glance. Do not lower that cutoff to squeeze out more

232
00:19:16,234 --> 00:19:16,445
runtime. Below three volts you are trading cell life for a couple of minutes of bubbles.

233
00:19:17,755 --> 00:19:50,077
Step seven. Fit the bubbler.

234
00:19:51,387 --> 00:19:55,492
First, understand what you actually have. The kit is not one object. It is a bottle,

235
00:19:55,552 --> 00:19:59,657
a cap with a one way valve, and a blower head, joined by silicone hose. There is about fifty millimetres

236
00:19:59,717 --> 00:20:03,822
of hose inside the bottle ending in a blue gravity ball, which sinks, so the pickup stays in solution

237
00:20:03,882 --> 00:20:07,988
at any arm angle, even inverted. Which means the heavy thing, the bottle, and the thing that must

238
00:20:08,048 --> 00:20:12,153
point past your fingertips, the blower head, do not have to be in the same place.

239
00:20:12,213 --> 00:20:16,318
That opens up a much better mount, if the blower can pull solution far enough.

240
00:20:17,629 --> 00:20:20,996
Now the hose test. Twenty minutes, one length of three by five silicone tube,

241
00:20:21,056 --> 00:20:24,422
and it decides your whole mount design. Do it the day the kit arrives, before any of the soldering.

242
00:20:24,482 --> 00:20:27,849
Assemble the kit as supplied and confirm it makes bubbles. Then replace the outside hose with about

243
00:20:27,909 --> 00:20:31,275
a hundred and fifty millimetres of tube, and run it again, held roughly the way your arm will hold

244
00:20:31,335 --> 00:20:34,702
it. The question is simply, does it still make bubbles at the same rate.

245
00:20:36,012 --> 00:20:41,518
If it passes, put the bottle on the forearm and the blower head at the knuckles,

246
00:20:41,578 --> 00:20:47,085
pointing past your fingertips. The bubbles still appear to come from your fingers,

247
00:20:47,144 --> 00:20:52,651
so the illusion is intact, and the heaviest item on the arm moves off the end of the lever where it

248
00:20:52,711 --> 00:20:58,217
was costing you the most. This is the better build if you can get it.

249
00:20:59,527 --> 00:21:05,572
If it fails, put both on the back of the hand, exactly as the kit intends, nozzle past the fingertips.

250
00:21:05,632 --> 00:21:11,677
It works as shipped with no modification and no risk. You just manage the weight instead.

251
00:21:11,737 --> 00:21:17,782
Run the bottle half full, mount the body back toward the wrist with the cap forward to shorten the

252
00:21:17,842 --> 00:21:23,887
lever arm, and anchor it to a wrist strap. A glove will not hold two hundred grams swinging for an

253
00:21:23,947 --> 00:21:29,992
evening.

254
00:21:31,302 --> 00:21:31,662
One job on the bubbler that is not cosmetic. The kit ships the motor on a P H two point zero female

255
00:21:31,722 --> 00:21:32,083
plug that mates straight to the cell. Cut it off and fit an S M two pin instead.

256
00:21:32,143 --> 00:21:32,503
If you leave it, the cell can be plugged directly into the motor, bypassing the MOSFET entirely,

257
00:21:32,563 --> 00:21:32,924
and then the trigger does nothing. While you are there, use silicone wire for the motor run,

258
00:21:32,984 --> 00:21:33,344
because it flexes constantly, and give the bubbler a quick release strap. Nothing about its mount

259
00:21:33,404 --> 00:21:33,765
should bridge the wrist, or the glove cannot come off without removing the bottle first.

260
00:21:35,075 --> 00:22:00,578
Step eight. Flash the firmware.

261
00:22:01,888 --> 00:22:09,243
Install the Arduino I D E, then add E S P thirty two board support through Boards Manager,

262
00:22:09,303 --> 00:22:16,658
searching for esp thirty two and installing the Espressif package. Then install the Fast L E D library

263
00:22:16,718 --> 00:22:24,073
from Library Manager. Select the X I A O E S P thirty two C three as your board,

264
00:22:24,133 --> 00:22:31,488
open the sketch, plug in U S B C and upload. If the upload fails, hold the boot button while plugging

265
00:22:31,548 --> 00:22:38,903
the cable in, to force bootloader mode.

266
00:22:40,213 --> 00:22:41,150
Before you upload, edit the three layout constants at the top of the sketch to match your own measurements.

267
00:22:41,210 --> 00:22:42,147
Forearm pixels, hand pixels, and gap pixels. Gap pixels is the measured pixel to pixel distance across

268
00:22:42,207 --> 00:22:43,144
the finished umbilical, connector body included, which is most of it, divided by one point six seven

269
00:22:43,204 --> 00:22:44,142
centimetres. About eight centimetres of umbilical gives you five. Guessing at this number is exactly

270
00:22:44,202 --> 00:22:45,139
what makes the comet look like it stumbles at the wrist. And flash the identical sketch to both arms.

271
00:22:45,199 --> 00:22:46,136
There is deliberately no left or right setting. As long as pixel zero is at the elbow on each arm,

272
00:22:46,196 --> 00:22:47,133
the mirroring is physical only.

273
00:22:48,443 --> 00:23:09,973
Step nine. Tune the timing. This is the step that makes the whole effect work,

274
00:23:10,033 --> 00:23:31,562
and it can only be done on the finished arm.

275
00:23:32,872 --> 00:23:33,142
The goal is that the comet reaches your fingertips at the exact moment the first bubble appears.

276
00:23:33,202 --> 00:23:33,472
When it is right, the light looks like it is causing the bubbles. When it is wrong by even a tenth

277
00:23:33,532 --> 00:23:33,801
of a second, it looks like lag. So film yourself firing it in slow motion. Any modern phone does a

278
00:23:33,861 --> 00:23:34,131
hundred and twenty or two hundred and forty frames a second. Watch it back frame by frame.

279
00:23:34,191 --> 00:23:34,461
If the comet arrives before the bubbles, increase comet travel milliseconds to slow it down.

280
00:23:34,521 --> 00:23:34,791
If it arrives after, decrease it. Reflash, film again. Expect three or four rounds.

281
00:23:34,851 --> 00:23:35,120
The default is two hundred and fifty milliseconds, and if you built the forearm bottle mount,

282
00:23:35,180 --> 00:23:35,450
expect a slightly longer number, because a longer feed hose takes marginally longer to prime.

283
00:23:36,760 --> 00:24:08,833
Step ten. Mount it into the glove and the sleeve.

284
00:24:10,144 --> 00:24:15,621
Hiding the strip under fabric is a genuine upgrade, not a compromise, because the fabric diffuses

285
00:24:15,681 --> 00:24:21,159
the pixels into one smooth continuous streak instead of visible dots. But it only works if the fabric

286
00:24:21,219 --> 00:24:26,697
cooperates. Thin, pale and stretchy glows beautifully. Thick or dark, especially leather,

287
00:24:26,756 --> 00:24:32,234
swallows almost all of it. So before you commit, hold a powered section of strip under the actual

288
00:24:32,294 --> 00:24:37,772
glove, in a dark room. If you cannot see it clearly, no firmware setting will rescue it.

289
00:24:37,832 --> 00:24:43,309
Get a different glove. And test the sleeve at the same time.

290
00:24:44,618 --> 00:24:50,264
If you are building the lace version from the design board, this section is overridden.

291
00:24:50,324 --> 00:24:55,971
Lace is open mesh. Light passes straight through the holes, so the strip stays visibly a strip,

292
00:24:56,031 --> 00:25:01,677
a hard bright line with dots, rather than a glow. You can lean into that, because circuitry under

293
00:25:01,737 --> 00:25:07,384
something delicate is on theme. Or you can add a diffuser layer underneath,

294
00:25:07,444 --> 00:25:13,090
thin white organza, tulle, or a frosted silicone channel, which gives you the soft glow with the lace

295
00:25:13,150 --> 00:25:18,797
pattern silhouetted on top. It is a taste call and it is cheap to test both.

296
00:25:18,857 --> 00:25:24,503
Do it with a scrap, before the gloves are cut into.

297
00:25:25,814 --> 00:25:26,016
Route the strip across the back of the hand, over the knuckles. Never the palm.

298
00:25:26,076 --> 00:25:26,278
That is where the trigger is and where you grip things. Stop short of the fingertips,

299
00:25:26,338 --> 00:25:26,541
because the nozzle needs that space and finger joints flex too much for strip to survive.

300
00:25:26,601 --> 00:25:26,803
Sew a fabric channel for the strip to sit in rather than gluing it down flat,

301
00:25:26,863 --> 00:25:27,065
so it can slide a little as your hand flexes. Glued taut strip tears itself off,

302
00:25:27,125 --> 00:25:27,327
or tears the glove. And the connector is never the anchor. Tack the cable to the garment on both sides

303
00:25:27,387 --> 00:25:27,590
of every plug, so a snag pulls the tack rather than the latch. Leave a service loop either side.

304
00:25:27,650 --> 00:25:27,852
Finally, a smear of dielectric grease in each connector shell keeps soap residue from corroding the

305
00:25:27,912 --> 00:25:28,114
contacts.

306
00:25:29,423 --> 00:26:20,924
Step eleven. Seal it against the soap.

307
00:26:22,234 --> 00:26:24,193
The build used to assume the pod sits on your upper arm, above the spray. That holds on a bench and

308
00:26:24,253 --> 00:26:26,212
fails at a rave, where your arms go up. Point a bubble gun at the ceiling for four hours and the pod

309
00:26:26,272 --> 00:26:28,230
is no longer above anything. Assume every surface gets wet, from every angle,

310
00:26:28,290 --> 00:26:30,249
all night. Arms overhead means solution runs down the arm, straight at the pod,

311
00:26:30,309 --> 00:26:32,268
into anything that faces up. The blower atomises solution, so a fine soapy mist settles on everything

312
00:26:32,328 --> 00:26:34,287
within a metre. And here is the one people miss: wicking. Soap film creeps along the wire insulation,

313
00:26:34,347 --> 00:26:36,306
into a connector shell and out the other end, hours after the splash that started it.

314
00:26:36,366 --> 00:26:38,324
Sealing the box is not enough if the wires are a wick. Dried bubble solution is also mildly conductive

315
00:26:38,384 --> 00:26:40,343
and it pulls moisture back out of the air, so a dry looking residue across two pins is a leakage path

316
00:26:40,403 --> 00:26:42,362
that comes back every humid night until you clean it off.

317
00:26:43,672 --> 00:27:04,548
Chasing a watertight box is the wrong target. You cannot get there with an F D M print,

318
00:27:04,608 --> 00:27:25,483
a U S B port and six wires leaving the case, and if you did, condensation would defeat you from the

319
00:27:25,543 --> 00:27:46,419
inside. Aim for this instead. Nothing that gets in can pool on a board. Everything that gets in has

320
00:27:46,478 --> 00:28:07,354
a way out at the bottom. And everything that gets in dries between events.

321
00:28:08,664 --> 00:28:10,324
Print the pod in P E T G, not P L A. P L A goes soft in a hot car and it is brittle exactly where

322
00:28:10,384 --> 00:28:12,043
a strapped on part flexes. Four perimeters, one point six millimetre walls minimum,

323
00:28:12,103 --> 00:28:13,763
because thin F D M walls leak through the layer lines themselves. Give it a lid with a gasket groove,

324
00:28:13,823 --> 00:28:15,483
two millimetre silicone cord in the groove, and four screws into heat set inserts.

325
00:28:15,543 --> 00:28:17,202
Exact sizes, because they matter here: the inserts are brass, four point six millimetre outside diameter

326
00:28:17,262 --> 00:28:18,922
by five millimetre long. The screws are M three by eight millimetre button heads,

327
00:28:18,982 --> 00:28:20,641
in stainless, not the black carbon steel ones the kit comes with. Model the bosses at four millimetres

328
00:28:20,701 --> 00:28:22,361
across and six deep, with at least two millimetres of plastic all round them,

329
00:28:22,421 --> 00:28:24,081
and drill the lid three point four for clearance. Set the inserts with a soldering iron at two hundred

330
00:28:24,141 --> 00:28:25,800
and forty degrees, going in square, until the top sits flush. Nothing sticky,

331
00:28:25,860 --> 00:28:27,520
because you will be opening this. Every opening faces down or aft. Nothing on the top surface.

332
00:28:27,580 --> 00:28:29,239
Then put a two millimetre drain hole at the lowest corner, and a small vent at the opposite high corner

333
00:28:29,300 --> 00:28:30,959
so it can breathe. Yes, that is a hole in your waterproof box. It is the difference between a box

334
00:28:31,019 --> 00:28:32,679
that drains and a box that holds a puddle against your protoboard. Wires leave through a grommet on

335
00:28:32,739 --> 00:28:34,398
the underside, and every one of them gets a drip loop: a downward loop below the entry,

336
00:28:34,458 --> 00:28:36,118
so water running along the insulation drips off instead of tracking in.

337
00:28:37,428 --> 00:28:49,303
The enclosure is the first line, not the only one. A thin coat of clear acrylic conformal spray over

338
00:28:49,363 --> 00:29:01,238
the assembled protoboard turns a soaked board into one you rinse, dry and keep using.

339
00:29:01,298 --> 00:29:13,174
Mask before you spray: the U S B C connector, the boost module's trimpot, the MOSFET tab,

340
00:29:13,234 --> 00:29:25,109
every connector housing, and the microswitch. Two thin coats beat one thick one.

341
00:29:25,169 --> 00:29:37,044
And do it after the bench test passes, not before, because reworking a coated board is miserable.

342
00:29:38,354 --> 00:29:40,935
The cell is the part that hurts you if this goes wrong, so it gets its own compartment,

343
00:29:40,995 --> 00:29:43,576
with a wall between it and the electronics. A leaking or vented cell must not take the board with

344
00:29:43,636 --> 00:29:46,217
it, and soap that gets in during a swap must not reach the brain. Nothing in this compartment is soldered.

345
00:29:46,277 --> 00:29:48,858
The cell arrives on its own factory pigtail, so there are no tabs and no joints in here to work loose.

346
00:29:48,918 --> 00:29:51,499
That is most of the reason this build uses a printed cradle instead of a bought sled.

347
00:29:51,559 --> 00:29:54,140
Nothing metal loose in that compartment either, ever. And check the cell's own wrap before every event.

348
00:29:54,200 --> 00:29:56,781
A nicked eighteen six fifty sleeve exposes the can, and the can is the negative terminal over the

349
00:29:56,841 --> 00:29:59,422
whole body of the cell. That is how a cell shorts against something it is only resting on.

350
00:29:59,482 --> 00:30:02,063
Re wrap or bin any that are torn. They cost almost nothing. The cradle grips the cell itself,

351
00:30:02,123 --> 00:30:04,704
never the pigtail. If the cell can shift, the pigtail takes the load and the crimp is what eventually

352
00:30:04,764 --> 00:30:07,345
fails. A foam pad or a printed rib that pinches the wrap is enough. And the lid closes positively,

353
00:30:07,405 --> 00:30:09,986
and opens without a tool.

354
00:30:11,296 --> 00:30:16,749
A bare lever microswitch in your soapy palm, under a glove, is not a sealed part.

355
00:30:16,809 --> 00:30:22,261
The cheapest fix is a printed pocket with a nitrile or silicone membrane over the lever.

356
00:30:22,321 --> 00:30:27,774
A scrap of a nitrile glove, stretched and glued around the rim, passes the press through and keeps

357
00:30:27,833 --> 00:30:33,286
the liquid out. On the connectors: grease every shell, wrap each mated plug in a turn of self amalgamating

358
00:30:33,346 --> 00:30:38,799
silicone tape, and point the plugs down. A shell facing up is a cup. And the wrist five pin sits above

359
00:30:38,859 --> 00:30:44,311
the cuff, not under it, because a cuff funnels solution straight into the plug.

360
00:30:45,621 --> 00:30:46,495
Then test it, before you trust it. Assemble the arm, seal it, and power it,

361
00:30:46,555 --> 00:30:47,429
because an unpowered box tells you nothing about tracking or shorts. Hold it overhead,

362
00:30:47,489 --> 00:30:48,363
the way you would actually fire it. Spray it all over with the real bubble solution for a full minute,

363
00:30:48,423 --> 00:30:49,298
from above, from the sides, and at the connectors. Fire the trigger a dozen times through the wetting.

364
00:30:49,358 --> 00:30:50,232
Leave it ten minutes, still powered, still wet. Then open it. Look for water inside,

365
00:30:50,292 --> 00:30:51,166
and look for water tracking along the inside of the wire entry. Either one means you move the entry

366
00:30:51,226 --> 00:30:52,100
point or add a drip loop.

367
00:30:53,410 --> 00:31:07,235
Step twelve. Build the second arm. It is a repeat of steps one to eleven, with two things to be careful

368
00:31:07,294 --> 00:31:21,119
about.

369
00:31:22,429 --> 00:31:22,860
Pixel zero goes at the elbow on this arm too. It is tempting to mirror the wiring along with the physical

370
00:31:22,920 --> 00:31:23,350
build. Do not. Identical wiring means identical firmware, which means one thing to maintain instead

371
00:31:23,410 --> 00:31:23,841
of two. And keep the same five pin and six pin conventions. If one arm puts the trigger on pins four

372
00:31:23,901 --> 00:31:24,331
and five and the other puts it on one and two, then your spares are not spares,

373
00:31:24,391 --> 00:31:24,822
and one wrong plug at an event costs you a board. Write the pinout on a bit of tape inside each pod.

374
00:31:26,131 --> 00:32:04,294
Step thirteen. Assemble the costume.

375
00:32:05,605 --> 00:32:08,267
With both arms built, run a checkout before any event. Cells out of both pods.

376
00:32:08,327 --> 00:32:10,988
Mate every connector and count them, four per arm, plus the Z H two at each microswitch.

377
00:32:11,048 --> 00:32:13,710
Cells in, and both arms should show the idle glow. Fire each hand separately.

378
00:32:13,770 --> 00:32:16,431
The left trigger drives the left arm only, the right drives the right only.

379
00:32:16,491 --> 00:32:19,153
If one trigger fires the other arm, you have crossed something, because there is no cross arm wiring

380
00:32:19,213 --> 00:32:21,874
in this design at all. Then fire both at once and watch for either arm dimming or glitching.

381
00:32:21,934 --> 00:32:24,596
They are electrically independent, so they should not interact. Then run both for the length of a

382
00:32:24,656 --> 00:32:27,317
bottle of solution. That is your real duty cycle.

383
00:32:28,627 --> 00:32:34,312
Getting into it. Order matters, and cells go in last, always. Pods onto the upper arms and strapped,

384
00:32:34,372 --> 00:32:40,057
with the cells out. Sleeves on, and mate the elbow six pin and two pin on each side.

385
00:32:40,117 --> 00:32:45,802
Gloves on, and mate the wrist five pin. Bubblers strapped on, mate the wrist two pin,

386
00:32:45,862 --> 00:32:51,547
bottles filled. Then cells in, and check both idle glows before you walk out the door.

387
00:32:52,856 --> 00:32:55,723
Getting out is the reverse, and it is the whole point of the build. Cells out first.

388
00:32:55,783 --> 00:32:58,650
Both arms are now dead and safe to unplug. Wrist two pin and the bubbler strap,

389
00:32:58,710 --> 00:33:01,578
and the bottles go somewhere upright. Wrist five pin, and the gloves peel off.

390
00:33:01,638 --> 00:33:04,505
Elbow six pin and two pin, and the sleeves come off. Then the pod straps. Four releases per arm,

391
00:33:04,565 --> 00:33:07,432
and none of them need a second person or a flat surface.

392
00:33:08,742 --> 00:33:15,694
Here is the test that matters. Can you get out of it alone, in a bathroom, with soapy hands,

393
00:33:15,754 --> 00:33:22,705
in under a minute? If you cannot, then something is still soldered that should not be,

394
00:33:22,765 --> 00:33:29,717
or a strap is fighting a connector. Fix that before the event, not at it.

395
00:33:31,027 --> 00:33:45,243
Afterwards. Cells out for storage, always. Never store the costume with cells connected.

396
00:33:45,303 --> 00:33:59,518
Rinse the blower head and the cap in warm water, because dried bubble solution glues the one way valve

397
00:33:59,578 --> 00:34:13,794
shut. Leave the silicone sleeve on the cap protrusion so the bottle does not empty into your bag.

398
00:34:13,854 --> 00:34:28,069
And wipe soap residue off any connector that got sprayed, then re grease it.

399
00:34:29,379 --> 00:34:32,389
Most faults in this build come from a short list. If nothing lights at all,

400
00:34:32,449 --> 00:34:35,458
check your common ground, your boost output, and that data is going into the input end of the strip.

401
00:34:35,518 --> 00:34:38,528
If it worked yesterday and not today, check every latch, because a half seated plug looks mated.

402
00:34:38,588 --> 00:34:41,598
If pixel zero died after a reconnect, the strip was plugged in live. If the comet jumps at the wrist,

403
00:34:41,658 --> 00:34:44,667
your gap pixels number does not match reality. And if the motor is weak and the MOSFET is hot,

404
00:34:44,727 --> 00:34:47,737
your gate is being driven at three point three volts, or the part is not logic level.

405
00:34:47,797 --> 00:34:50,806
Four newer ones worth knowing. If the blower twitches every time you power up,

406
00:34:50,866 --> 00:34:53,876
your gate pulldown is missing. If the elbow pixel is double blinking and the trigger does nothing,

407
00:34:53,936 --> 00:34:56,946
that is the low voltage shutoff: swap the cell. If your laptop complains about U S B power,

408
00:34:57,006 --> 00:35:00,015
D two is missing or backwards. And if an arm is completely dead with a freshly charged cell,

409
00:35:00,075 --> 00:35:03,085
check the disconnect switch before you suspect the board. The full table is in the build manual.

410
00:35:04,395 --> 00:35:07,235
Last thing. Your field kit. Carry a pre made spare wrist umbilical and one spare S M pigtail pair

411
00:35:07,295 --> 00:35:10,136
of each size. Because of the connector design, both swap without a soldering iron.

412
00:35:10,196 --> 00:35:13,036
Carry the kit charger and a power bank too. Also carry extra bubble solution.

413
00:35:13,096 --> 00:35:15,936
It runs out long before the battery does. And know the two failures you cannot fix.

414
00:35:15,996 --> 00:35:18,836
A flat cell, and a dead trigger. There is one of each per arm and both are in use,

415
00:35:18,896 --> 00:35:21,737
because no spares were bought. Protected cells and lever microswitches both sell in multi packs,

416
00:35:21,797 --> 00:35:24,637
so that is a cheap gap to close before the next event.

417
00:35:25,948 --> 00:35:30,048
And that is the build. Work one arm at a time, bench test before you sew anything into a glove,

418
00:35:30,108 --> 00:35:34,209
and keep the cell out of the pod whenever you are plugging things together.

419
00:35:34,269 --> 00:35:38,369
Full detail is in the build manual, the circuit itself is drawn in the schematic,

420
00:35:38,429 --> 00:35:42,530
the reasoning behind every part is in the parts list, and the costume direction is in the design document.

421
00:35:42,590 --> 00:35:46,690
Go make some bubbles.
